'''2†.''' To reckon or compute; count.
{{smaller block|The motion of the sun whereby years are ''accounted''.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err.}}}}
'''3.''' To assign or impute; give the credit of; reckon as belonging or attributable. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Even as Abraham believed God, and it was ''accounted'' to him for righteousness.
{{right|Gal. ii. 6.}}}}
{{smaller block|You have all sorts of graces ''accounted'' to you.
{{right|''Jerrold'', Works, IV. 408.}}}}
'''4†.''' To give an account, reason, or explanation of; explain.
{{smaller block|A way of ''accounting'' the solidity of ice.
{{right|''Glanville.''}}}}
'''5†.''' To take into consideration. ''Chaucer.''—'''6†.''' To recount; relate. ''Chaucer.''

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To render an account or relation of particulars; answer in a responsible character: followed by ''with'' or ''to'' before a person, and by ''for'' before a thing: as, an officer must ''account with'' or ''to'' the treasurer ''for'' money received.
{{smaller block|They must ''account to'' me ''for'' these things, which I miss so greatly.
{{right|''Lamb'', Old Benchers.}}}}
'''2.''' To furnish or assign a reason or reasons; give an explanation: with ''for'': as, idleness ''accounts for'' poverty.
{{smaller block|You’ll not let me speak—I say the lady can ''account for'' this much better than I can.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Rivals, iv. 2.}}}}
'''3†.''' To reckon; count.
{{smaller block|Calendar months, . . . by which months we still ''account''.
{{right|''Holder'', On Time.}}}}

